<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<assembly manifestVersion="1.0" xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v1">

  <!--
    ADR-25. This file exists for one setting, and the rest of it is the minimum Windows needs
    to read that setting.

    WPF applications are SYSTEM DPI aware by default - measured on this product before this
    file existed, by asking the running window through GetAwarenessFromDpiAwarenessContext
    rather than by reading documentation. Under system awareness Windows renders the window at
    the DPI the session logged on with and BITMAP-STRETCHES it onto any monitor scaled
    differently. Stretched text is blurry in the literal sense, and no amount of font tuning
    inside the application can undo it.

    It changes nothing on the machine this was written on - one monitor, system DPI 144,
    monitor DPI 144, nothing to stretch. It changes everything on a second monitor at a
    different scale, on a scale changed without signing out, and on a laptop leaving a dock.
    All three are ordinary for an administrator, which is who this tool is for.

    Sources, because a claim about the platform needs one:
      https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/hidpi/high-dpi-desktop-application-development-on-windows
      https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/hidpi/declaring-managed-apps-dpi-aware
      https://github.com/dotnet/wpf/issues/859

    BOTH elements below are needed and they are not duplicates. dpiAware is the old setting and
    is what Windows 8.1 and Windows 10 before the Creators Update read. dpiAwareness is the
    newer one and wins where it is understood. A manifest carrying only the second is system
    aware on older Windows, which is inside the floor ADR-5 sets.
  -->
  <application xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:asm.v3">
    <windowsSettings>
      <dpiAware xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/SMI/2005/WindowsSettings">true/pm</dpiAware>
      <dpiAwareness xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/SMI/2016/WindowsSettings">PerMonitorV2</dpiAwareness>
    </windowsSettings>
  </application>

  <!--
    The supported-OS list. Without it Windows applies compatibility shims for an application it
    assumes predates Windows 10, and one of those shims is DPI virtualisation - which would
    quietly undo the two lines above. The floor is ADR-5, Windows 10 1809 and Server 2019, so
    only the Windows 10 and 11 entry belongs here.
  -->
  <compatibility xmlns="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:compatibility.v1">
    <application>
      <supportedOS Id="{8e0f7a12-bfb3-4fe8-b9a5-48fd50a15a9a}" />
    </application>
  </compatibility>

</assembly>
